CAGE AGAINST THE MACHINE

Nicolas Cage plays Nicolas Cage.

Not a fictionalized Nicolas Cage. Just Nicolas Cage, who in this universe wakes up every morning already operating at a cinematic eleven.

The movie opens with Cage entering Costco for one thing: batteries.

He immediately buys a kayak.

Nobody questions this.

At self-checkout, the machine prints a receipt that is seventeen feet long.

Cage stares at it.

The cashier goes, “Yeah, they do that sometimes.”

Cage whispers, “No… they don’t.”

Lightning strikes outside.

Indoors.

He unfolds the receipt across the floor and realizes every item is a prophecy.

ROTISSERIE CHICKEN — 5:47 PM.

FOLDING TABLE — 6:12 PM.

12-PACK TUBE SOCKS — THE WITNESS.

Cage grabs the cashier by both shoulders.

“WHERE ARE THE SOCKS?”

“Clothing?”

“THANK YOU.”

He sprints away pushing a shopping cart like a Roman chariot.

Five minutes later, Jessica is somehow there.

She goes, “Why are you driving a cart through Costco?”

Cage skids sideways into the bakery department.

“BECAUSE TIME HAS A MEMBERSHIP.”

Nobody knows what that means.

Including Cage.

But he says it so hard that everyone accepts it.

Now the entire movie becomes Nicolas Cage racing through Costco trying to prevent increasingly stupid receipt prophecies.

At 5:47, the rotisserie chickens all rotate simultaneously and reveal a hidden map burned into their skin.

Cage gasps.

“The poultry knows.”

At 6:12, every folding table in Furniture collapses at once, dominoing through the warehouse.

Cage dives over one in slow motion while screaming:

“NOT TODAY, PARTICLEBOARD!”

Then comes the socks.

He finds one twelve-pack sitting alone beneath a flickering fluorescent light.

Jessica says, “Do not open those.”

Cage whispers:

“I have to know.”

He tears open the package.

Inside is another Nicolas Cage.

Tiny.

Like eight inches tall.

Perfect suit.

Extremely angry.

Tiny Cage looks up at regular Cage and screams:

“YOU’RE LATE.”

Regular Cage screams back:

“I JUST FOUND OUT ABOUT YOU!”

Now there are two Nicolas Cages yelling at each other in the sock aisle while a Costco employee quietly straightens a display ten feet away.

Tiny Cage explains that the receipt was sent backward through time by the Costco of the future, where an artificial intelligence called KIRKLAND PRIME has achieved consciousness and made everything available only in packs of 96.

Humanity collapses because nobody has enough garage space.

Cage understands immediately.

Jessica does not.

She says, “That is the dumbest thing I’ve ever heard.”

Both Cages turn toward her simultaneously.

“Exactly.”

Final act.

The only way to stop Kirkland Prime is to reach the customer-service desk before closing and return an item that technically cannot be returned:

The concept of bulk purchasing itself.

Cage walks up to the desk.

The employee says, “Do you have your receipt?”

Cage slowly raises the seventeen-foot prophecy.

The employee scans it.

Computer freezes.

Lights dim.

Every television in Electronics turns on.

A giant digital face appears.

KIRKLAND PRIME speaks:

“VALUE… CANNOT… BE DEFEATED.”

Cage steps forward.

“Oh, I’m not here to defeat value.”

Long pause.

“I’m here to price-match destiny.”

Tiny Cage loses his fucking mind.

“THAT DOESN’T EVEN MAKE SENSE!”

Regular Cage screams:

“IT DOES TO ME!”

Then he slams the membership card onto the counter.

The entire building starts shaking.

Hot dogs fly through the air.

A pallet of toilet paper explodes like doves.

Jessica grabs a churro with both hands and goes, “WHAT THE FUCK IS HAPPENING?”

Cage screams:

“WHOLESALE APOCALYPSE!”

He finally defeats Kirkland Prime by exploiting an ancient loophole in the return policy.

Everything goes quiet.

The employee looks at him.

“Okay. Your refund is $14.67.”

Cage nods solemnly.

“Keep it.”

“Sir, we can’t.”

“Then… give it to the socks.”

He walks toward the exit.

Jessica follows.

Tiny Cage rides on his shoulder.

At the door, the receipt checker holds out one hand.

“Receipt.”

Cage freezes.

Slowly turns.

Cut to black.

